sermo, User-facing announcements
================================

This file summarises changes from the user's point of view.
For the full technical history, see CHANGELOG.en.md.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.8.2
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Patch release. Sixty job scripts and four toolboxes, played on the nine
ports, showed where the ports differed from gtk3sermo.

* Sixty ready-to-run job scripts (examples/metiers, sermo-doc package):
  five per job, from system administration to local web. By default they
  print the command instead of running it.

* Four toolboxes (examples/boites-a-outils, sermo-doc package): system,
  server, administrator and user, ten tools each. Same rules: dry run by
  default, confirmation before deleting.

* The package checksums are signed: SHA256SUMS.asc, with the haplo-dialog
  key (devel@haplo-dialog.fr, fingerprint
  66C7 F4AD 7A58 A037 967F  8339 3799 9F99 7570 F1ED). The public key is on
  the website; the check is described in the README.

* Both MCP servers can be installed: sermoia-mcp and sermoman-mcp packages.

* XML entities (&amp; &lt; &gt; &quot; &apos; &#NN;) are decoded: a script
  that escapes its values gets the original value back. A value written on
  several lines keeps all of them.

* On the nine ports, as in gtk3sermo: a list's first row is chosen only if
  the list takes the focus; <default> chooses the row; the first button of a
  radio group is checked; <text> returns the text shown;
  <tree exported-column> returns the column asked for.

* gtk3sermo no longer sorts a <table> when it opens: the document order is
  kept (sorting in auto-sort, or on a click on a header).

* On all nine ports, boxes are laid out as in gtk3sermo: equal cells
  (homogeneous), stretching set on the box, buttons in a row packed to the
  right. In a button, the icon and the label are centred.

* --do receives EXIT (the button clicked). In serve mode, htmlsermo passes
  it the values typed in the browser.

* ncursessermo timers, fltk1sermo decimals, efl1sermo fields (no more
  "&laquo;") fixed. fltk1sermo keeps an item containing "/"
  ("Europe/Paris") instead of turning it into a submenu. In a terminal,
  ncursessermo leaves its output variables on screen.

* Icons: the ports without GTK look them up as GTK does (theme, Adwaita,
  hicolor, symbolic variant); gtk4, qt6, efl1 and lvgl show the ones that
  were missing. htmlsermo's page takes the shape of the window; lvglsermo's
  tabs no longer cut their labels.

* sermoia-mcp: the preview (sermo_render) no longer runs any command of the
  dialog; each tool has a timeout; examples can be searched with a phrase;
  documentation rewritten, in French and English.
  sermoman-mcp: the sermo_scripts tool gives the inventory of the scripts.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.8.1
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Patch release: widgets drawn wrong, seen on the website screenshots.

* <tree> is shown in columns, under its headers, on every port (no longer
  "a|b|c" on one line).

* <statusbar> shows its <default>; <text> shows what <input> read.

* lvglsermo now draws <edit>, <calendar>, PNG images, toggle buttons,
  links, wizards and stacks one page at a time. A search field no longer
  damages the dialog's memory.

* fltk1sermo, qt6sermo and efl1sermo: --render-png returns code 0.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.8.0
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Minor release: nine ports instead of seven.

* htmlsermo writes the dialog into a standalone HTML page instead of
  opening a window; it needs no graphics library. With
  SERMO_HTML_MODE=serve, the page is served locally and returns the
  entered values.

* lvglsermo draws with LVGL: Wayland, X11, or a framebuffer on request —
  for kiosks and screens without a desktop.

* sermoia-mcp, a second MCP server, helps an AI write, check and see a
  dialog.

* Package recipes for Arch, Fedora (RPM), Gentoo, Slackware, Alpine and
  NixOS, in packaging/autres-os/.

* HISTOIRE.en.md tells the history of gtkdialog and its maintainers.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.7
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Patch release.

* A progress bar whose command printed a line starting with a NUL byte made
  sermo read one byte outside its memory. Fixed (gtk3 and gtk4 ports).

* gtk4 port: two windows open at the same time with the same variable no
  longer overwrite each other silently, and a widget's default value is
  visible to a neighbouring widget's command — as in gtk3.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.6
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Security patch release.

* With a list of allowed commands (SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS), a script could still
  get code loaded by naming a variable LD_PRELOAD. Fixed: such variables are
  no longer passed to the command.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.5
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Security patch release.

- A <table> or a <tree> whose row held many "|" characters (127 or
  more) could make sermo write outside its memory. This is fixed. When
  that content came from a command or an external file, it was a
  security risk.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.4
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Patch release.

- In gtk4, a <calendar> could set the wrong month depending on the day
  the dialog was opened: on 30 September, a <default> of 2000-02-29 gave
  2000-09-29. Month ends and 29 February were affected. It is fixed; the
  other ports were right.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.3
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

First published 2.x release. Versions 2.0.0 to 2.7.2 stayed internal. If
you come from 1.x, also read 2.7.2 below and MIGRATION.en.md.

Patch release: what an <input> reads now has a limit.

- Each <input>, command or file, reads at most 16 MiB. Beyond that, reading
  stops, and a warning goes to standard error. The SERMO_INPUT_MAX
  environment variable changes the limit, in bytes; 0 removes it. Until now,
  an endless command such as "yes" made the dialog grow until memory ran out.
- The progress bar is not capped: it follows its command to the end. In gtk3
  and gtk4, it no longer grows on a fast command, and no longer brings the
  dialog down when SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS refuses its command.
- In gtk3 and gtk4, <edit> no longer brings the program down on a very large
  file.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.2
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Stayed internal, like versions 2.0.0 to 2.7.1: the first published 2.x
release is 2.7.3.

If you come from 1.x, what changes for you (details in MIGRATION.en.md):

- The packages are renamed: sermo-backend-gtk3, -gtk4, -qt6 and sermo-gtkdialog
  replace gtk3sermo, gtk4sermo, qt6sermo and gtksermo.
  "apt install ./sermo-backend-gtk3_2.7.2-3_amd64.deb" removes the 1.x package
  it replaces. The commands keep their names.
- HAPLO_ALLOWED_CMDS and HAPLO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK become SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS and
  SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK. The old names are still read, with a warning.
- SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS compares paths: listing "echo" no longer allows "/bin/echo"
  when PATH finds /usr/bin/echo.
- Four more ports (fltk1sermo, efl1sermo, sdl3sermo, ncursessermo) and a "sermo"
  command that points to one of the installed ports.
- Building goes through CMake and requires VTE (gtk3, gtk4) and gtk-layer-shell
  (gtk3). The Arch, Gentoo, RPM and Slackware recipes are no longer provided.

Compared with 2.7.1, internal:

A fix release. It repairs what windows actually SHOW: until now nothing checked
that — the benches read the values a dialog returns, never its picture.

What changes for you:

- Every port finally says its own name. "qt6sermo --version" used to answer
  "sermocore version 2.7.1"; it now answers "qt6sermo version 2.7.2". Word
  positions do not move: a script reading the 3rd word for the version keeps
  working.

- Dialogs lay out correctly on qt6 and sdl3. A column meant to expand stayed
  tiny, a bottom band could fail to appear at all, and on sdl3 a 560x420 window
  could come out three times too tall and empty.

- The progress bar: the manual claimed a value between 0.0 and 1.0. That is
  WRONG, and its example produced an empty bar. A bar expects a PERCENTAGE,
  0 to 100: write "echo 75", not "echo 0.75".

- Building from source now installs the result: "make install" lays down the
  binary, its manual page and its icons for all seven ports. Six of them had no
  install rules at all.

- The user manual and the developer manual now exist in English.

Two things to know, written down plainly after measurement:

- The two execution guards (SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS and SERMO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK) do
  NOT cover <terminal>. That widget opens a /bin/sh through VTE, outside the
  core: a dialog containing one hands out a shell, even with both variables
  set. Do not deploy such a dialog where you believe those variables define the
  boundary. See SECURITY.en.md. In the same vein, --include sends every command
  through /bin/sh -c, so it runs nothing at all once a command list is set.
- Closing the window with the cross returns no value: the whole output is
  EXIT="abort". The values are printed only when the dialog ends through a
  button (an EXIT: action). The manual promised the opposite.

The user manual has been reworked: six syntaxes it gave as examples were
refused by the parser (among them <okbutton/>, which does not exist: it is
<button ok></button>). All 54 of its XML examples now pass.

Windows finally carry the project icon: the HD logo, shipped by the packages at
the eight theme sizes (16 to 256 px). No icon was installed until now, except for
qt6. The ncurses port writes "HD" in its top frame instead: a terminal cannot
carry an icon. It no longer announces itself as the SDL 3 port.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.1
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

An internal release. Compared with 2.7.0:

The terminal and Wayland anchoring are back, and <input> reads what it is given
everywhere.

- <terminal> opens a real terminal again with gtk3sermo and gtk4sermo: it had
  been missing since 2.0.0, without a single message.
- gtk3sermo anchors its windows under Wayland again (the layer, edge, dist and
  reserve attributes of <window>), with a compositor that allows it.
- <input> and <input file> fill entry, text, edit, comboboxtext, list, tree and
  table on all seven ports, with the same values, and the command runs once. On
  qt6, fltk1, efl1, sdl3 and ncurses, some of these widgets read nothing, or ran
  the command twice.
- sdl3sermo no longer crashes on a <table> without <label>.
- <chooser> no longer aborts qt6sermo, fltk1sermo, efl1sermo, sdl3sermo and
  ncursessermo: a dialog containing one did not open at all. On those ports it
  becomes a button that opens the toolkit's file selector; gtk3sermo and
  gtk4sermo keep the in-window selector.
- Debian packages: sermo-backend-gtk3, -gtk4, -qt6, -fltk1, -efl1, -sdl3,
  -ncurses, sermo-gtkdialog and sermo-core-dev. The 1.x packages (gtk3sermo,
  gtk4sermo, qt6sermo, gtksermo) move to 2.x with a plain upgrade.
- <combobox> is no longer filled by <input> on qt6, fltk1 and efl1: gtk3 never
  did it. Use <comboboxtext>.
- To build gtk3 and gtk4: libvte-2.91-dev, libvte-2.91-gtk4-dev and
  libgtk-layer-shell-dev.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.7.0
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

The progress bar moves on all seven ports, and gtk3sermo and gtk4sermo load
interfaces designed with Glade again.

- <progressbar> follows its command on qt6, fltk1, efl1, sdl3 and ncurses as on
  gtk3 and gtk4: the bar moves line by line, and the action set for 100 runs.
- --glade-xml=FILE loads a GtkBuilder (Glade) interface file with gtk3sermo and
  gtk4sermo; the other ports refuse the option and say so.
- --include=functions.sh works with a relative path: under /bin/sh it loaded
  nothing.
- A <frame> shows all its children on fltk1, efl1, sdl3 and ncurses, not only
  the first.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.8
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Benches that said OK without checking anything no longer do.

- sdl3 and ncurses: clicking the font chooser closed the window. Fixed.
- Several test benches answered OK without checking anything: they now fail
  when they verify nothing.
- To contribute: ci/construire.sh and ci/bancs.sh build the seven ports and run
  every bench, exactly like the repository's automatic verification.
- --glade-xml has no effect in this edition (no Glade library exists for
  GTK 3 or 4); the man pages said otherwise.
- The EFL development package is libefl-all-dev.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.7
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Protections that were announced now really apply.

- FORTIFY_SOURCE did not work in the core, which was built without
  optimisation: it now does.
- SERMO_ALLOWED_CMDS can no longer be bypassed by a program of the same name
  placed elsewhere. Beware: a command written with a path must now be listed
  under that path, or be the one PATH finds.
- The 1.x names, HAPLO_ALLOWED_CMDS and HAPLO_NO_SHELL_FALLBACK, are read again.
- In a terminal (ncurses), the password shows as stars, text fields can finally
  be filled, and the OK button closes the dialog.
- --version shows the right number.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.6
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Nothing new on screen. 586 source files announced a version in their header that
was no longer the right one - and eighty of them even carried the product's
former name.

The number is not updated, it is removed: a source file has no reason to carry
one.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.5
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

On fltk1, opening an expander scrambled the window: the label below climbed on
top of the title and the content spilled out of its frame. Fixed - neighbouring
widgets are pushed down, and the window grows with them.

The open-state arrow is back too: the port used a symbol that does not exist in
FLTK, so nothing was drawn.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.4
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

On fltk1, opening an expander ERASED its title: only the arrow was left. Fixed -
the title now survives every open and close.

<expander> also enters the manual, where it was missing entirely: the state it
exports (true or false), the expanded= attribute that opens it at startup, and
the two ways of writing its title. The reference served to AI tools said the
opposite of what the parser does; it is corrected.

⚠ Still on fltk1, opening an expander scrambles the layout of the neighbouring
widgets. That is not repaired.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.3
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

<expander> now opens and collapses as it should on all seven ports. The
expanded="true" attribute was read by NONE of the five non-GTK ports: two of
them always collapsed, the other three always opened.

An expander with no attribute is collapsed - that is the reference's rule, and
it now holds everywhere. The three ports that read <default> were answering a
syntax that exists nowhere else.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.2
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

<frame> now renders its title, on all seven ports. A script reading a frame's
variable received an empty string everywhere except on gtk3 and gtk4 - and
nothing flagged it.

The defect surfaced while writing the two bench cases the language's largest
containers were missing, <frame> and <notebook>: both shipped everywhere,
neither measured. <notebook> was already correct.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.1
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Nothing new on screen. One core function was redeclared by hand in four ports
(gtk3, gtk4, qt6, fltk1), with a type different from the one in its header. The
compiler only complained about one of them; the defect was in all four. They
now include the header, as efl1 already did. What you see on screen does not
change.

The roadmap no longer announces a "web" backend. The door stays open to other
rendering engines - it is the door the ncurses port came through - but the
product announces only the seven ports it ships.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.6.0
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Three more tags, on all seven ports.

<flowbox> lays its children along a line, then wraps to the next. <overlay>
stacks them: the first is the background, the following ones float above it -
the only container in the language that superimposes. <revealer> shows or hides
its child.

The limits are stated, not left unsaid. gtk3 and gtk4 genuinely reflow as the
window shrinks; the other ports use fixed columns, and only gtk3 and gtk4 know
which child is selected. The <revealer> animation exists on gtk3 and gtk4 only:
elsewhere the port shows or hides, without a transition. A terminal does not
superimpose, so ncurses draws the background then the layers one below the
other, each preceded by a marker.

These three tags had been accepted for a long time but produced NOTHING outside
gtk4: a script using them got a silently truncated window, without a word of
warning.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.5.0, Four more tags
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

<toolbar> arranges buttons in a row that calls itself a toolbar. <stack> shows
N pages, one at a time, without a <notebook>'s tabs; one attribute is enough to
add the navigation buttons. <wizard> chains steps together with Previous, Next
and Finish. <menubutton> puts a menu where you are, not only at the top of the
window.

All four work on the seven ports. None of them is borrowed as-is from a
toolkit: GTK 4 removed both the assistant and the toolbar, and Qt's QWizard is
a window, not something you place inside a box. They are therefore COMPOSED,
the same way everywhere, so that the same script gives the same result.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.4.0, One number, one spelling
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

The number a script receives no longer depends on the machine's language, nor
on the port. It is written with a DOT, always: under a French locale,
<hscale digits="2"> used to render "2,50" - a script comparing "2.50"
recognised nothing any more.

The digits= attribute is now honoured by all seven ports: four of them rendered
"2" where the reference rendered "2,50". And <progressbar> no longer exports
anything at all, like the reference: it is a display, not an input.

If you were comparing values by hand to work around those discrepancies, you
can stop.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.3.1, The value that depended on the machine
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

<levelbar> rendered nothing on four ports, and anything at all on the others.
<drawingarea> was chatty: one port rendered its dimensions, two others the word
"canvas", where the reference renders nothing.

Worse and quieter: the value written depended on the machine's LANGUAGE. The
same script, on the same binary, rendered "0,5" here and "0.5" there. That is
repaired for writing as well as for reading.

Also fixed: a <progressbar> with no attribute at all made the FLTK port stop.
The most ordinary tag there is.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.3.0, The handle
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

<paned> splits the window into two areas separated by a handle YOU drag. An
<hbox> freezes the split when the script is written; a <paned> lets it move.
Attributes: orientation, position (in pixels or as a percentage), resizable.

Exactly two children. A third one is refused with a message: it is never
swallowed in silence.

On all seven ports, including in the terminal, where the boundary moves with
the arrow keys.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.2.0, A grid, at last
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

<grid columns="N"> lays children out in flow - in script order, wrapping every
N - and ALIGNS the columns from one row to the next.

Until now the language had 62 widget types and only TWO layout containers,
<hbox> and <vbox>: a two-column form was written by stacking <hbox>, and each
row sized itself on its own. The labels never lined up with one another.

Careful: <grid> is not <table>. <table> is the column list of data inherited
from gtkdialog.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.1.1, The tags that were pretending
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Five tags were accepted while doing nothing, depending on the port: <eventbox>
lost its child, <linkbutton> and <pulse> returned an empty value, <image> was
short-circuited, and <filechooser> returned its default value without ever
letting anything be CHOSEN.

They now work everywhere. The <filechooser> opens the system's native selector
wherever the toolkit provides one.

None of those tags was measured by a bench: that is why the defect lasted so
long. They are measured now.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.1.0, The house put in order
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

Nothing new on screen: this version puts the house in order. qt6 sheds 9,198
lines that served no purpose, a clone of the repository finally builds without
looking for anything next to it, and every file now says who it belongs to.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────
Version 2.0.0, Modularisation
──────────────────────────────────────────────────

sermo splits into two parts: a shared core and one backend per graphical
library. In practice you install the backend of your choice
(“sermo-backend-gtk3”, “-fltk1”, “-qt6”…): each backend is self-contained
(the core is statically linked in), there is nothing else to install.

The main benefit is lightness. Installing the FLTK backend no longer pulls in
GTK or Qt — each backend depends only on its own toolkit. On a minimal machine,
that means a handful of dependencies instead of dozens.

The same XML script gives the same interface on all seven backends (gtk3, gtk4,
qt6, fltk1, efl1, sdl3 and ncurses — the last one, in the terminal, is the
lightest). The “sermo” alias points at the default backend; scripts that call
“gtkdialog” keep working thanks to the optional “sermo-gtkdialog” package (GTK 3
backend).

Each backend now follows your system theme (light or dark).

All seven backends render exactly the same values as the reference port
(24 cases out of 24). The last GTK 4 discrepancies have been fixed.
